    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access — Exploit Public-Facing Application", "technique": "T1190", "what_happened": "At 2027-09-14 02:11:07 CEST, Suricata logged the first 'ET WEB_SPECIFIC_APPS SQL Injection Attempt' alert with data.srcip=45.137.21.134 hitting data.url=/api/v1/products?id=... on the public-facing host 10.108.57.177. The first request carried a boolean-true probe payload (a classic AND '1'='1 tautology) — the canonical injectability confirmation step that precedes any blind SQLi data extraction.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signature 'ET WEB_SPECIFIC_APPS SQL Injection Attempt' triggered with data.srcip=45.137.21.134 against data.url containing '/api/v1/products' and data.http.method=GET. Univariate signature: single external source + SQL metacharacters in a query-string parameter against a public web endpoint.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T02:11:07+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Initial Access — DBMS Fingerprinting", "technique": "T1190", "what_happened": "Within the same minute the same data.srcip=45.137.21.134 sent follow-up requests to the same /api/v1/products endpoint carrying VERSION()-based and UNION SELECT payloads — the attacker is fingerprinting the backend DBMS (MySQL) and probing column counts. Suricata fired additional 'ET WEB_SPECIFIC_APPS SQL Injection Attempt' and 'ET WEB_SPECIFIC_APPS Possible SQL Injection UNION SELECT' alerts in close succession.", "chain_reason": "Same data.srcip + same data.url path, with data.http payload escalating from a tautology probe to VERSION() / UNION SELECT — the canonical fingerprint-then-enumerate progression. The signature_id set widened from the generic SQLi signature to the UNION SELECT specific signature.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T02:12:33+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Initial Access — Schema Enumeration", "technique": "T1190", "what_happened": "The attacker then issued UNION SELECT requests pulling information_schema.tables — enumerating the database schema to locate the credentials table. The data.url query parameter contained 'information_schema.tables' and 'table_name'. Suricata kept firing SQL injection signatures against the same single endpoint from the same single source.", "chain_reason": "Same data.srcip + same data.url, payload now references information_schema — a database-internal metadata table that has no legitimate use in a normal product-catalog API call. This is unambiguous schema enumeration, not application traffic.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T02:13:40+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Initial Access / Collection — Blind Boolean/Time-Based Extraction", "technique": "T1190", "what_happened": "At 02:14:51 CEST the attacker switched to a blind extraction payload: an IF(ASCII(SUBSTRING((SELECT password FROM users WHERE id=1),1,1))>64,SLEEP(2),0) construct. This is byte-by-byte blind SQLi — each request reveals one bit/byte of the 'users.password' column via a measurable response-time delta. The endpoint is the same /api/v1/products. A trailing stacked-query payload ('1; DROP TABLE users--') was also observed. Suricata fired SQL injection signatures plus a possible time-based blind SQLi signature.", "chain_reason": "Same data.srcip + same data.url, payload now contains SLEEP() and SUBSTRING()/ASCII() — the signature of blind boolean/time-based extraction. The progression probe → fingerprint → schema enum → blind extraction across a 3m44s window from a single source against a single public endpoint is the canonical Exploit-Public-Facing-Application SQLi kill chain.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T02:14:51+0200"}
